1
00:00:40,791 --> 00:00:44,466
RABBI: Savree maranun verabonun verobotay

2
00:00:44,586 --> 00:00:47,965
Baruch atah Adonai eloheynu melech ha-olam

3
00:00:48,298 --> 00:00:51,177
Boray p'ree ha-gafen

4
00:00:51,343 --> 00:00:54,768
Baruch atah Adonai eloheynu melech ha-olam

5
00:00:54,846 --> 00:00:56,974
A-sher kid'shanu b'mitzvo-tav

6
00:00:57,099 --> 00:00:59,522
V'ratza va-nu v'shabbat kaddsho

7
00:00:59,601 --> 00:01:01,979
B'ahavah oov-ratzone hin-cheelanu

8
00:01:02,145 --> 00:01:06,399
Zee-karon Pma-ah-say Way-sheet kee hoo yom

9
00:01:06,482 --> 00:01:08,326
T'cheelah Fmikraay kodesh

10
00:01:08,527 --> 00:01:11,656
Zaycher leetzeeat mitzrayim

11
00:01:11,822 --> 00:01:15,167
Kee vanu vachatah v'ohtahnu keydashta mekol ha-ahmim

12
00:01:15,325 --> 00:01:17,919
V'shabbat kadd-shcha b'ahavah oov-ratzone

13
00:01:17,995 --> 00:01:19,497
Hin-chal-ta-nu

14
00:01:19,788 --> 00:01:25,636
Baruch atah, Adonai m'kaddaysh ha-Shabbat

15
00:01:37,681 --> 00:01:40,935
(TRAIN WHISTLE BLOWING)

16
00:02:22,042 --> 00:02:23,139
Name?

17
00:02:23,560 --> 00:02:24,903
Horowitz, Salomon.

18
00:02:24,978 --> 00:02:29,199
(MANY PEOPLE TALKING INDISTINCTLY)

19
00:02:29,374 --> 00:02:30,876
Schneider, Sarah.

20
00:02:31,001 --> 00:02:32,403
MAN 1: Birnbaum, Olga.

21
00:02:32,486 --> 00:02:35,911
(CHILDREN CRYING)
(INDISTINCT TALKING)

22
00:02:35,989 --> 00:02:37,616
- Over there. Over there!
- Kommen Sie doch.

23
00:02:39,409 --> 00:02:40,410
Isak Hudes.

24
00:02:43,580 --> 00:02:45,298
Zucker, Helena.

25
00:02:45,499 --> 00:02:47,593
MAN 2: Zucker, Helena.

26
00:02:48,293 --> 00:02:49,590
Hirsch, Salomon?

27
00:02:49,711 --> 00:02:51,008
Hirsch, Salomon.

28
00:02:51,213 --> 00:02:52,886
Hauptman, Chaim!

29
00:02:53,006 --> 00:02:54,724
Weisman! Weisman, Marcus!

30
00:02:54,925 --> 00:02:56,268
Feber, Ludwig!

31
00:02:56,385 --> 00:02:57,762
MAN 3: Feber, Ludwig.

32
00:02:57,844 --> 00:02:59,266
Elsa Bauman.

33
00:03:01,348 --> 00:03:03,316
- Josef Klein.
- MAN 4: Klein.

34
00:03:03,475 --> 00:03:04,772
Davidowich, lgnacy.

35
00:03:04,893 --> 00:03:06,190
Paula Biffer.

36
00:03:06,269 --> 00:03:07,486
Nadel, Rachela.

37
00:03:08,105 --> 00:03:10,153
- Steiner, Gertruda.
- Steiner, Hilda.

38
00:03:11,274 --> 00:03:14,904
(GLOOMY SUNDAY PLAYING)

39
00:04:22,512 --> 00:04:24,105
(ORCHESTRA PLAYING)

40
00:04:24,181 --> 00:04:26,525
(GUESTS CHATTERING INDISTINCTLY)

41
00:04:30,979 --> 00:04:32,856
(WHISPERING)

42
00:05:09,351 --> 00:05:12,150
MAITRE'D: - Jerzy, you know who that man is?
- I don't know.

43
00:05:13,897 --> 00:05:15,194
(WOMAN LAUGHING)

44
00:05:15,398 --> 00:05:17,446
(ORCHESTRA CONTINUES)

45
00:05:30,997 --> 00:05:33,020
Let's get together, please.

46
00:05:34,084 --> 00:05:35,536
Smile. Good.

47
00:06:07,117 --> 00:06:08,494
Yes, sir.

48
00:06:10,453 --> 00:06:12,706
Bring them over a round of drinks.

49
00:06:14,240 --> 00:06:18,045
Very good, sir. And who shall I say they are from?

50
00:06:19,796 --> 00:06:21,890
You can say they are from me.

51
00:06:42,994 --> 00:06:44,146
From the gentleman.

52
00:06:44,470 --> 00:06:45,422
Where?

53
00:06:51,527 --> 00:06:52,455
Do you know him?

54
00:06:55,290 --> 00:06:56,758
Find out who he is.

55
00:06:56,833 --> 00:06:58,035
Yes, sir.

56
00:07:01,838 --> 00:07:05,593
Agnieszka, I would give anything to hear you sing tonight.

57
00:07:05,675 --> 00:07:07,518
- (SIGHS)
- But I know you won't.

58
00:07:07,594 --> 00:07:08,595
(LAUGHS)

59
00:07:10,597 --> 00:07:12,091
You embarrass me.

60
00:07:21,524 --> 00:07:22,467
What's he doing?

61
00:07:26,279 --> 00:07:27,206
Stay here.

62
00:07:32,226 --> 00:07:33,328
How are you doing?

63
00:07:33,411 --> 00:07:36,381
You'd leave a woman alone at the table in a place like this?

64
00:07:38,291 --> 00:07:40,644
Sweetheart, you're the picture of loneliness.

65
00:07:41,186 --> 00:07:42,087
Oh.

66
00:07:42,755 --> 00:07:46,304
Mmm, what a lovely fragrance. You are breaking my heart.

67
00:07:46,383 --> 00:07:48,852
An extra chair, please. Vodka for my friend.

68
00:07:48,927 --> 00:07:50,053
And for the lady?

69
00:07:50,478 --> 00:07:51,270
Pernod.

70
00:07:51,346 --> 00:07:54,350
Im Grunewald, im Grunewald ist Holzauktion

71
00:07:54,432 --> 00:07:56,810
(MEN WHISTLING AND LAUGHING)

72
00:07:56,935 --> 00:07:57,936
Ist Holzauktion

73
00:07:58,061 --> 00:08:01,406
Im Grunewald, im Grunewald ist Holzauktion

74
00:08:02,232 --> 00:08:04,735
MAN: Come to the table, girls! Drink with us!

75
00:08:04,818 --> 00:08:05,819
Ist Holzauktion

76
00:08:05,944 --> 00:08:07,992
Links um die Ecke 'rum, rechts um die Ecke 'rum

77
00:08:08,071 --> 00:08:10,995
Überall ist heute Holzauktion

78
00:08:11,074 --> 00:08:12,371
Come to us! Marry us!

79
00:08:15,453 --> 00:08:17,330
(WHISPERING)

80
00:08:17,414 --> 00:08:20,543
Der ganze Klafter Süßsholz kostet 'nen Taler

81
00:08:20,625 --> 00:08:23,720
'nen Taler, 'nen Taler

82
00:08:23,837 --> 00:08:26,761
Der ganze Klafter Süßholz kostet 'nen Taler

83
00:08:26,840 --> 00:08:28,092
'nen Taler. ..

84
00:08:32,161 --> 00:08:33,058
Thank you.

85
00:08:33,179 --> 00:08:34,780
I'll tell you what I mean by cooperative.

86
00:08:34,931 --> 00:08:36,558
Two days after the law is passed

87
00:08:36,683 --> 00:08:38,276
that all Jews have to wear the star,

88
00:08:38,351 --> 00:08:40,091
the Jewish tailors are turning them out by the gross

89
00:08:40,137 --> 00:08:42,610
in a variety of fabrics at three zloty each.

90
00:08:42,856 --> 00:08:44,429
Tell me about your cellar wines.

91
00:08:44,607 --> 00:08:47,862
- I have an excellent German Riesling. 1937.
- Mmm...

92
00:08:48,086 --> 00:08:51,080
- French. A Bordeaux. Château Latour, '28, '29?
- No, I'm sorry.

93
00:08:51,740 --> 00:08:54,118
It's as if they have no idea what kind of law it is.

94
00:08:54,200 --> 00:08:56,669
As if it's the emblem of a riding club.

95
00:08:56,870 --> 00:08:58,590
- A Margaux, '29?
- No, no, we don't have that.

96
00:08:58,663 --> 00:09:01,257
- Burgundy then? A Romanée-Conti, '37?
- Yes.

97
00:09:01,624 --> 00:09:04,252
It's human nature. "We'll do this to avoid that."

98
00:09:04,502 --> 00:09:07,051
That's what they have done since thousands of years.

99
00:09:07,714 --> 00:09:09,216
It's what they do. They weather the storm.

100
00:09:09,299 --> 00:09:12,929
But this storm is different.
This is not the Romans. This storm is the SS.

101
00:09:13,011 --> 00:09:16,811
Wem Gott will rechte Gunst erweisen

102
00:09:16,973 --> 00:09:20,318
Den schickt er in die weite Welt

103
00:09:20,935 --> 00:09:24,610
Wem Gott will rechte Gunst erweisen

104
00:09:24,814 --> 00:09:28,159
Den schickt er in die weite Welt

105
00:09:28,568 --> 00:09:31,697
Faleri, Falera

106
00:09:31,863 --> 00:09:35,618
Faleri
Falera-ha ha ha

107
00:09:35,700 --> 00:09:39,079
Faleri, Falera

108
00:09:39,245 --> 00:09:42,485
Den schickt er in die weite Welt

109
00:09:44,300 --> 00:09:45,201
Martin.

110
00:09:46,169 --> 00:09:47,112
Yes, sir.

111
00:09:48,505 --> 00:09:49,757
Who is that man?

112
00:09:51,883 --> 00:09:54,102
That's Oskar Schindler!

113
00:09:54,594 --> 00:09:55,641
Faleri

114
00:09:56,012 --> 00:09:57,855
Falera-ha ha ha

115
00:09:58,306 --> 00:10:01,401
Den schickt er in die weite Welt

116
00:10:01,851 --> 00:10:05,105
Faleri, Falera

117
00:10:05,588 --> 00:10:07,691
Den schickt er in die weite Welt

118
00:10:07,774 --> 00:10:09,367
(ALL CHEERING) To Krakow!

119
00:10:12,195 --> 00:10:14,197
(SINGING SONG IN GERMAN)

120
00:10:26,960 --> 00:10:28,633
(SOLDIERS LAUGHING)

121
00:10:41,808 --> 00:10:43,526
(SINGING CONTINUES)

122
00:10:47,313 --> 00:10:49,566
MAN ON LOUDSPEAKER: An alle Juden!

123
00:10:49,816 --> 00:10:53,937
Ab sofort ist es alien Juden untersagt...

124
00:10:53,979 --> 00:10:56,702
Fleisch auf koschere Art zuzubereiten.

125
00:10:58,074 --> 00:11:00,998
Meldung an alle Juden. Ab sofort...

126
00:11:21,347 --> 00:11:24,483
- Rabbi... Private property cannot be confiscated.
- How long are the schools going to be closed?

127
00:11:24,559 --> 00:11:27,353
- I do not know.
- "Article 47, pillage is formally prohibited."

128
00:11:27,437 --> 00:11:28,814
You don't know anything!

129
00:11:28,938 --> 00:11:30,656
- I am familiar with the Hague Convention.
- Religious...

130
00:11:30,940 --> 00:11:34,940
They come into our house
and tell us we don't live there anymore.

131
00:11:35,403 --> 00:11:38,026
It now belongs to a certain SS officer.

132
00:11:38,239 --> 00:11:40,458
Please. I only know what they tell me.

133
00:11:40,575 --> 00:11:43,454
And what they tell me changes from day to day.

134
00:11:43,620 --> 00:11:46,064
Aren't you supposed to be able to help?

135
00:11:46,623 --> 00:11:49,467
- I...
- I mean, what if I just took this thing off?

136
00:11:50,043 --> 00:11:51,465
What are they going to do about it?

137
00:11:51,628 --> 00:11:53,000
They will shoot you.

138
00:11:53,029 --> 00:11:55,799
- Why don't you stop this silly talk?
- Itzhak Stern!

139
00:11:57,300 --> 00:11:59,769
I'm looking for Itzhak Stern.

140
00:12:02,013 --> 00:12:04,032
Are you Itzhak Stern or not?

141
00:12:05,308 --> 00:12:07,561
- I am.
- Where can we talk?

142
00:12:18,071 --> 00:12:21,166
There's a company you did the books for on Lipowa Street.

143
00:12:22,617 --> 00:12:24,210
Made what? Pots and pans?

144
00:12:24,661 --> 00:12:27,005
By law, I have to tell you, sir, I'm a Jew.

145
00:12:27,747 --> 00:12:30,692
Well, I'm a German. So there we are.

146
00:12:34,545 --> 00:12:35,842
A good company, you think?

147
00:12:36,005 --> 00:12:37,348
Modestly successful.

148
00:12:38,258 --> 00:12:40,352
I know nothing about enamelware. Do you?

149
00:12:40,468 --> 00:12:41,620
I was just the accountant.

150
00:12:43,513 --> 00:12:45,314
Simple engineering though, wouldn't you think?

151
00:12:45,390 --> 00:12:46,767
Change the machines around, whatever you do...

152
00:12:46,849 --> 00:12:48,577
You could make other things, couldn't you?

153
00:12:49,686 --> 00:12:54,032
Field kits, mess kits. Army contracts.

154
00:12:56,526 --> 00:12:59,075
Once the war ends, forget it, but for now it's great.

155
00:12:59,153 --> 00:13:00,996
You can make a fortune, don't you think?

156
00:13:01,072 --> 00:13:04,121
I think most people right now have other priorities.

157
00:13:05,159 --> 00:13:07,082
- Like what?
- (SIGHS)

158
00:13:08,037 --> 00:13:10,238
I'm sure you'll do just fine once you get the contracts.

159
00:13:10,248 --> 00:13:12,125
In fact, the worse things get, the better you'll do.

160
00:13:12,208 --> 00:13:15,712
Well, I can get the signatures I need. That's the easy part.

161
00:13:16,254 --> 00:13:19,033
Finding the money to buy the company, that's hard.

162
00:13:19,966 --> 00:13:21,468
You don't have any money?

163
00:13:21,551 --> 00:13:25,431
Not that kind of money. You know anybody?

164
00:13:27,557 --> 00:13:29,901
Jews, yeah. Investors.

165
00:13:30,852 --> 00:13:32,854
You must have contacts in the Jewish business community,

166
00:13:32,937 --> 00:13:33,938
working here.

167
00:13:34,022 --> 00:13:36,616
What community? Jews can no longer own businesses.

168
00:13:36,691 --> 00:13:37,988
That's why this one's in receivership.

169
00:13:38,109 --> 00:13:39,406
But they wouldn't own it.

170
00:13:39,527 --> 00:13:42,531
I'd own it. I'd pay them back in product, pots and pans.

171
00:13:42,864 --> 00:13:43,907
Pots and pans.

172
00:13:44,024 --> 00:13:45,667
Something they can use.

173
00:13:46,409 --> 00:13:47,831
Something they can feel in their hands.

174
00:13:47,910 --> 00:13:49,912
They can trade it on the black market,
do whatever they want.

175
00:13:50,038 --> 00:13:51,006
Everybody's happy.

176
00:13:53,916 --> 00:13:56,020
If you want, you could run the company for me.

177
00:14:01,382 --> 00:14:02,635
Let me understand.

178
00:14:03,260 --> 00:14:05,558
They'd put up all the money, I'd do all the work.

179
00:14:06,137 --> 00:14:09,266
What, if you don't mind my asking, would you do?

180
00:14:09,932 --> 00:14:12,026
I'd make sure it's known the company's in business.

181
00:14:12,101 --> 00:14:14,229
I'd see that it had a certain panache.

182
00:14:14,312 --> 00:14:16,956
That's what I'm good at, not the work. Not the work.

183
00:14:18,691 --> 00:14:19,984
The presentation.

184
00:14:39,170 --> 00:14:42,891
I'm sure I don't know anybody who'll be interested in this.

185
00:14:44,217 --> 00:14:47,346
(EXHALES) Well, they should be, Itzhak Stern.

186
00:14:48,471 --> 00:14:50,645
Tell them they should be.

187
00:15:00,650 --> 00:15:02,493
MAN ON LOUDSPEAKER:
Achtung! Achtung!

188
00:15:02,910 --> 00:15:08,538
An alle Juden. Ab sofort ist es verboten...

189
00:15:09,342 --> 00:15:13,839
PRIEST RECITING: Vere dignum et justum est, aurum et salutary,

190
00:15:14,122 --> 00:15:17,968
no's tibia semper et unique gratis agree:

191
00:15:18,326 --> 00:15:22,097
Domino sancta, Pater omnipotent, sauterne Deus...

192
00:15:22,171 --> 00:15:25,846
I've got a client who'll sell marks for zloty at 2.45-to-1.

193
00:15:25,925 --> 00:15:27,802
Wool, mink collar. It's a nice coat.

194
00:15:28,127 --> 00:15:29,804
She'll trade it for ration coupons.

195
00:15:29,887 --> 00:15:31,560
Wicks, for lamps.

196
00:15:31,681 --> 00:15:33,399
You have a truckload of wicks?

197
00:15:33,474 --> 00:15:35,772
What am I going to do with a truckload of wicks?

198
00:15:35,852 --> 00:15:37,354
Nine by 12, nine by 14.

199
00:15:37,895 --> 00:15:40,023
I don't know. Big, beautiful, Persian.

200
00:15:44,193 --> 00:15:45,194
Past.

201
00:15:45,378 --> 00:15:46,325
What?

202
00:15:46,404 --> 00:15:48,281
- You don't recognize this?
- It's shoe polish.

203
00:15:48,364 --> 00:15:49,661
In metal containers?

204
00:15:49,982 --> 00:15:51,079
You asked for shoe polish.

205
00:15:51,159 --> 00:15:53,127
- In metal containers. This is glass.
- In metal containers.

206
00:15:53,202 --> 00:15:54,242
It's not what I asked for.

207
00:15:54,287 --> 00:15:56,360
- What's the difference?
- What's the difference?

208
00:15:56,381 --> 00:15:57,673
- What's the difference?
- OTHERS: Shah!

209
00:15:57,748 --> 00:15:59,375
My client sold it to his client,

210
00:15:59,542 --> 00:16:01,135
who sold it to the German army.

211
00:16:01,210 --> 00:16:02,203
Only by the time it got there,

212
00:16:02,246 --> 00:16:04,119
because of the freezing cold, it broke.

213
00:16:04,346 --> 00:16:05,719
All 10,000 units.

214
00:16:05,840 --> 00:16:08,138
- Mmm, ooh, ooh, ooh.
- This is not my problem.

215
00:16:08,217 --> 00:16:09,764
- This is not his problem.
- This is not your problem?

216
00:16:09,844 --> 00:16:11,061
This is not my problem.

217
00:16:11,720 --> 00:16:12,672
All right.

218
00:16:13,890 --> 00:16:16,564
The German army wants to find out where it came from.

219
00:16:17,310 --> 00:16:18,762
I am going to make sure they find out.

220
00:16:18,828 --> 00:16:19,980
Now it's your problem.

221
00:16:20,229 --> 00:16:22,402
You, be quiet!
(CHUCKLES)

222
00:16:23,900 --> 00:16:25,368
Metal containers.

223
00:16:26,903 --> 00:16:27,995
Hello.

224
00:16:29,489 --> 00:16:32,333
Pardon me for interfering, but that's a nice shirt.

225
00:16:33,743 --> 00:16:35,211
Nice shirt.

226
00:16:36,245 --> 00:16:38,919
Do you know where I can find a nice shirt like that?

227
00:16:39,540 --> 00:16:43,135
PRIEST: ...laudare, benedicere et predicare.

228
00:16:46,797 --> 00:16:48,265
Like this?

229
00:16:48,382 --> 00:16:51,261
It's illegal to buy or sell anything on the street.

230
00:16:51,719 --> 00:16:54,268
We don't do that. We're here to pray.

231
00:16:56,516 --> 00:17:00,686
PRIEST: Jesum Christum
Dominum Nostrum...

232
00:17:00,686 --> 00:17:03,030
Do you have any idea how much a shirt like this costs?

233
00:17:03,105 --> 00:17:04,352
Nice things cost money.

234
00:17:04,382 --> 00:17:08,782
PRIEST: ...adorant Dominationes, tremunt Potestates.

235
00:17:09,045 --> 00:17:13,997
Coeli coelorumque virtutes ac Beata Seraphim

236
00:17:14,400 --> 00:17:18,250
socia exultatione concelebrant...

237
00:17:18,354 --> 00:17:19,697
How many?

238
00:17:21,832 --> 00:17:24,361
I'm going to need some other things,
too, as things come up.

239
00:17:25,044 --> 00:17:26,017
This won't be a problem.

240
00:17:26,337 --> 00:17:27,255
From time to time.

241
00:17:28,965 --> 00:17:31,309
MAN ON LOUDSPEAKER:
Achtung! Achtung!

242
00:17:55,324 --> 00:17:57,452
(CHILDREN CRYING)

243
00:18:13,843 --> 00:18:16,517
- OFFICER 1: Name? Adresse?
- OFFICER 2: Formular ausfüllen!

244
00:18:16,596 --> 00:18:19,224
- OFFICER 1: Weiter, weiter!
- OFFICER 2: Nicht drängeln!

245
00:18:19,432 --> 00:18:21,981
Kommen Sie hierher! SteHen Sie sich doch an!

246
00:18:29,191 --> 00:18:30,784
(SILVERWARE CLATTERING)

247
00:19:09,607 --> 00:19:11,075
MAN ON LOUDSPEAKER:
Achtung, Achtung!

248
00:19:11,192 --> 00:19:13,490
Alle Juden sind verpflichtet...

249
00:19:13,569 --> 00:19:16,789
im abgesonderten jüdischen Bezirk zu wohnen.

250
00:19:16,947 --> 00:19:19,666
Nichteinhaltung der Meldepflicht
bei der Einwohnerbehörde...

251
00:19:19,825 --> 00:19:23,875
verstößt gegen das Edikt 44/91 und führt zur Anzeige...

252
00:19:24,038 --> 00:19:25,961
beim Militärgericht.

253
00:19:28,918 --> 00:19:31,171
This is obviously the right place. How are you doing?

254
00:19:31,253 --> 00:19:33,255
(INDISTINCT SHOUTING)

255
00:19:35,883 --> 00:19:39,513
Goodbye, Jews! Goodbye, Jews!

256
00:19:39,595 --> 00:19:43,566
Goodbye, Jews!

257
00:19:46,268 --> 00:19:50,068
GIRL: Goodbye, Jews! Goodbye, Jews!

258
00:19:50,940 --> 00:19:53,034
Goodbye, Jews!

259
00:19:55,936 --> 00:19:58,615
This iz the vechteka, Moyshe.
Ojciec.

260
00:19:59,215 --> 00:20:00,942
Nyne, this kan neash zyane.

261
00:20:10,334 --> 00:20:11,586
(SIGHS)

262
00:20:11,669 --> 00:20:13,421
(BABY CRYING)
(WOMAN SINGING LULLABY IN POLISH)

263
00:20:13,504 --> 00:20:14,972
Oh, my God.

264
00:20:16,032 --> 00:20:18,552
Oy, God. Oh, my God.

265
00:20:26,033 --> 00:20:27,936
Oh, my God.

266
00:20:35,576 --> 00:20:37,074
It could not be better.

267
00:20:37,770 --> 00:20:39,038
It could be worse.

268
00:20:39,155 --> 00:20:41,057
How? Tell me.

269
00:20:41,323 --> 00:20:44,202
(SHOUTING) How on Earth could it possibly be worse?

270
00:20:44,285 --> 00:20:45,628
(SLAMS DOWN SUITCASE)

271
00:20:46,328 --> 00:20:47,671
MAN: Dzien dobry.

272
00:20:48,789 --> 00:20:50,917
- Dzien dobry.
- Dzien dobry.

273
00:20:51,167 --> 00:20:53,670
- Dzien dobry.
- Dzien dobry.

274
00:20:53,961 --> 00:20:54,962
Dzien dobry.

275
00:20:55,629 --> 00:20:57,051
(SPEAKING GERMAN)

276
00:20:59,341 --> 00:21:00,934
- Hey, Goldberg.
- Poldek.

277
00:21:01,010 --> 00:21:02,354
What's this?

278
00:21:03,471 --> 00:21:05,769
The Judenrat has its own police now.

279
00:21:05,848 --> 00:21:06,940
You don't say.

280
00:21:07,057 --> 00:21:08,104
Ordnungsdienst.

281
00:21:08,184 --> 00:21:11,438
I'm a policeman now, could you believe it?
It's hard to believe.

282
00:21:11,520 --> 00:21:13,013
No, it's not hard to believe.

283
00:21:13,522 --> 00:21:15,616
It's a good racket, Poldek. The only racket here.

284
00:21:15,691 --> 00:21:18,786
Look, maybe I could put in
a good word for you with my superiors.

285
00:21:19,028 --> 00:21:20,029
Your superiors?

286
00:21:20,446 --> 00:21:22,824
Come on, they are not as bad as everyone says.

287
00:21:23,574 --> 00:21:25,918
Well, they're worse than everyone says,

288
00:21:26,035 --> 00:21:28,379
but it's a lot of money. A lot of money.

289
00:21:29,789 --> 00:21:31,416
Give me my housing assignment.

290
00:21:32,541 --> 00:21:34,043
Come on.

291
00:21:34,168 --> 00:21:35,670
You look funny in that hat, Goldberg.

292
00:21:36,587 --> 00:21:37,588
(LAUGHS)

293
00:21:37,713 --> 00:21:40,016
You look like a clown, you know.

294
00:21:46,263 --> 00:21:49,187
STERN: He's a very important man.
Just give him two minutes of your time.

295
00:21:49,266 --> 00:21:52,691
Since when did time cost money? Just sit down and listen.

296
00:21:53,354 --> 00:21:55,002
Please. Sir?

297
00:21:58,809 --> 00:22:01,237
For each 1,000 you invest, I will repay you with...

298
00:22:01,320 --> 00:22:01,812
Two hundred.

299
00:22:01,812 --> 00:22:03,622
Two hundred kilos of enamelware a month,

300
00:22:03,697 --> 00:22:05,324
to begin in July and to continue for one year.

301
00:22:05,407 --> 00:22:06,611
After which time, we're even.

302
00:22:07,407 --> 00:22:09,011
That's it. It's very simple.

303
00:22:09,370 --> 00:22:10,547
Not good enough.

304
00:22:10,671 --> 00:22:12,014
It's not good enough.

305
00:22:12,289 --> 00:22:13,717
Not good enough?

306
00:22:14,667 --> 00:22:18,592
Look where you're living.
Look where you've been put. "Not good enough."

307
00:22:18,838 --> 00:22:21,032
A couple of months ago you'd be right. Not anymore.

308
00:22:21,549 --> 00:22:23,493
Money's still money.

309
00:22:26,428 --> 00:22:30,058
No, it is not. That's why we're here.

310
00:22:30,558 --> 00:22:32,401
Trade goods, that's the only currency

311
00:22:32,518 --> 00:22:34,361
that'll be worth anything in the ghetto.

312
00:22:34,436 --> 00:22:36,414
Things have changed, my friend.

313
00:22:39,608 --> 00:22:41,361
Did I call this meeting?

314
00:22:41,944 --> 00:22:43,742
You told Mr. Stern you wanted to speak to me.

315
00:22:43,821 --> 00:22:45,664
I'm here. I've made you a fair offer.

316
00:22:45,739 --> 00:22:49,789
Fair would be a percentage in the company.

317
00:22:50,703 --> 00:22:54,833
(LAUGHING) Forget the whole thing. Get out.

318
00:23:00,629 --> 00:23:03,098
How do we know that you will do what you say?

319
00:23:03,632 --> 00:23:05,225
Because I said I would.

320
00:23:05,301 --> 00:23:07,679
You want a contract? To be upheld by what court?

321
00:23:07,761 --> 00:23:09,934
I said what I'll do. That's our contract.

322
00:23:11,307 --> 00:23:13,480
(CONVERSING IN POLISH)

323
00:23:13,809 --> 00:23:14,810
...mielismy racje.

324
00:23:27,766 --> 00:23:32,044
Piec, szesc, siedem,

325
00:23:33,330 --> 00:23:35,923
osiem, dziewiec.

326
00:23:39,668 --> 00:23:41,295
(MACHINERY BUZZES)

327
00:23:45,507 --> 00:23:46,975
(SCHINDLER CLAPPING)

328
00:23:48,728 --> 00:23:52,724
The standard SS rate for Jewish
skilled laborers is seven marks a day,

329
00:23:53,098 --> 00:23:55,100
five for unskilled and women.

330
00:23:56,018 --> 00:23:59,142
This is what you pay the Reich Economic Office.

331
00:23:59,221 --> 00:24:01,319
The Jews themselves receive nothing.

332
00:24:02,191 --> 00:24:05,991
Poles you pay wages. Generally, they get a little more.

333
00:24:06,070 --> 00:24:07,413
Are you listening?

334
00:24:07,529 --> 00:24:08,906
What was that about the SS?

335
00:24:08,989 --> 00:24:10,866
The rate? The what?

336
00:24:11,367 --> 00:24:12,960
The Jewish worker's salary. You pay it

337
00:24:13,077 --> 00:24:14,670
directly to the SS, not to the worker.

338
00:24:14,745 --> 00:24:16,497
- He gets nothing.
- But it's less.

339
00:24:16,580 --> 00:24:18,215
It's less than what I would pay a Pole.

340
00:24:18,215 --> 00:24:19,132
It's less.

341
00:24:19,208 --> 00:24:23,964
That's the point I'm trying to make. Poles cost more.

342
00:24:24,838 --> 00:24:25,839
(SNIFFS)

343
00:24:25,923 --> 00:24:28,517
Why should I hire Poles?

344
00:24:29,510 --> 00:24:32,764
An enamelware factory over at Lipowa Street.

345
00:24:33,890 --> 00:24:36,312
It's owned by a German.

346
00:24:36,392 --> 00:24:40,397
But it's outside the ghetto,
so you can barter for extra goods,

347
00:24:40,479 --> 00:24:42,197
for eggs, I don't know what you need,

348
00:24:42,314 --> 00:24:43,987
with the Polish workers. You can't get it here.

349
00:24:44,066 --> 00:24:47,536
Also, he's asking for 10 healthy women for the...

350
00:24:47,611 --> 00:24:48,703
I am healthy.

351
00:24:48,779 --> 00:24:49,826
(CROWD LAUGHING)

352
00:24:49,905 --> 00:24:51,327
Bring your sister, okay?

353
00:24:51,407 --> 00:24:53,501
(SHOUTING ORDERS IN GERMAN AND POLISH)

354
00:24:56,954 --> 00:24:57,955
Chodz.

355
00:24:58,622 --> 00:25:00,302
You need to convince them you have a trade,

356
00:25:00,374 --> 00:25:02,001
something valuable to the war effort.

357
00:25:02,084 --> 00:25:03,510
- Like what?
- I'm a musician.

358
00:25:03,535 --> 00:25:04,461
What if you don't?

359
00:25:04,545 --> 00:25:05,392
Your name goes on a list.

360
00:25:05,413 --> 00:25:07,760
And they put you on trucks...

361
00:25:07,840 --> 00:25:09,842
- No, no, that's not true.
- You're a musician?

362
00:25:09,925 --> 00:25:10,597
Yeah.

363
00:25:10,660 --> 00:25:12,432
I'd find a good hiding place if I were you.

364
00:25:12,511 --> 00:25:14,479
I'm not going to hide like some animal.

365
00:25:14,996 --> 00:25:16,698
There are places to hide.

366
00:25:17,266 --> 00:25:18,438
(WHISTLE BLOWING)

367
00:25:22,646 --> 00:25:23,863
Der Nächste!

368
00:25:23,939 --> 00:25:26,283
I'm a graduate of the University of Lviv.

369
00:25:27,651 --> 00:25:30,370
I used to work at the hydroelectric station in Roznów.

370
00:25:30,446 --> 00:25:31,789
MAN: Blauschein.

371
00:25:35,951 --> 00:25:37,669
I'm gonna get the necessary papers

372
00:25:37,786 --> 00:25:39,538
because this carol is no good anymore.

373
00:25:39,621 --> 00:25:42,420
You can stay in this line all day, you'll still get one of these.

374
00:25:42,499 --> 00:25:46,254
They are no good. You'll have to leave the line now,

375
00:25:46,336 --> 00:25:48,509
then you'll get a blue card, a Blauschein,

376
00:25:48,630 --> 00:25:50,849
to say that you are an essential worker.

377
00:25:50,924 --> 00:25:53,848
I'll get these papers for you at Pankiewicz.

378
00:25:53,969 --> 00:25:55,642
Pharmacist. Just there, look.

379
00:25:56,764 --> 00:25:57,736
Guess who is here?

380
00:25:58,015 --> 00:26:00,814
GOLDBERG: Poldek! Enjoying the weather, Poldek?

381
00:26:00,893 --> 00:26:02,270
Enjoying the lines?

382
00:26:02,394 --> 00:26:03,737
Need some shoe polish?

383
00:26:03,812 --> 00:26:05,560
In a metal container maybe?
(GOLDBERG LAUGHS)

384
00:26:06,648 --> 00:26:10,903
Not essential? I think you
misunderstand the meaning of the word.

385
00:26:11,320 --> 00:26:13,866
No Blauschein, sir. Stand over there.

386
00:26:14,080 --> 00:26:15,166
Move. Next!

387
00:26:17,367 --> 00:26:19,495
What do you mean, "Not essential"?

388
00:26:20,120 --> 00:26:24,626
I teach history and literature.
Since when it's not essential?

389
00:26:30,347 --> 00:26:32,516
He's a metal polisher.

390
00:26:39,973 --> 00:26:40,700
It was not right?

391
00:26:40,700 --> 00:26:42,671
It's very good. He's 53 years old.

392
00:26:42,684 --> 00:26:43,506
It's too new.

393
00:26:43,528 --> 00:26:44,996
Mmm. Thank you.

394
00:26:53,779 --> 00:26:55,781
You're crazy. You left it in your drawer.

395
00:26:55,906 --> 00:26:57,954
How many times have I told you?

396
00:26:58,575 --> 00:27:01,499
Your work certificate you keep in your pocket at all times.

397
00:27:01,578 --> 00:27:05,048
And you leave yours in your drawer.
How many times I've told you?

398
00:27:06,708 --> 00:27:08,585
I'm a metal polisher.

399
00:27:17,928 --> 00:27:23,901
Take the round sheet of tin, dip it in soap emulsion,

400
00:27:25,394 --> 00:27:28,648
then place it centrally in the press.

401
00:27:44,872 --> 00:27:48,046
We obtain the soup pot. The soup pot.

402
00:27:48,750 --> 00:27:50,297
Soup pot.

403
00:27:52,254 --> 00:27:53,927
Grosz, Mrs. Grosz, stay together.

404
00:27:59,178 --> 00:28:02,272
Sir, I have 10 work permits here

405
00:28:02,330 --> 00:28:04,524
for the Deutsche Emailwarenfabrik

406
00:28:04,600 --> 00:28:07,103
cosigned by Mr. Bankier and myself.

407
00:28:07,686 --> 00:28:11,782
FOREMAN: if it's carbonized, clean it with a file.

408
00:28:12,816 --> 00:28:16,491
But don't touch both the electrodes at the same time.

409
00:28:17,154 --> 00:28:20,078
You'll get electrocuted. Here, have a try.

410
00:28:24,661 --> 00:28:26,959
Roll the metal under the press,

411
00:28:27,080 --> 00:28:29,503
but be very careful unless you want your fingers

412
00:28:29,583 --> 00:28:31,426
to become part of the teapot.

413
00:28:34,087 --> 00:28:35,060
Occupation?

414
00:28:35,081 --> 00:28:37,554
I'm a writer. I play the flute.

415
00:28:37,633 --> 00:28:41,183
But Moses is a skilled metalworker.

416
00:28:41,261 --> 00:28:43,529
He can make tin pots. He can make tanks.

417
00:28:43,547 --> 00:28:45,349
He can make whatever Mr. Schindler asks.

418
00:28:45,432 --> 00:28:49,812
He's highly skilled. Give him your card.
Moses, give him your card.

419
00:28:52,481 --> 00:28:56,907
FOREMAN: Now, dip the basin with the tongs into the enamel solution.

420
00:28:59,613 --> 00:29:03,834
And after swiveling it around,
carefully place it aside to drip dry.

421
00:29:07,162 --> 00:29:08,105
And whatever he asks you,

422
00:29:08,122 --> 00:29:10,965
allow me just to represent you and speak for you.

423
00:29:11,041 --> 00:29:12,509
Don't say a word.

424
00:29:13,001 --> 00:29:16,346
SCHINDLER: Filing, billing, keeping track of my appointments, shorthand.

425
00:29:16,463 --> 00:29:19,165
Typing, obviously. How is your typing?

426
00:29:19,490 --> 00:29:20,592
Uh, all right.

427
00:29:20,676 --> 00:29:21,544
Please.

428
00:29:29,810 --> 00:29:31,733
(TYPING SLOWLY)

429
00:29:35,190 --> 00:29:36,658
(TYPING SLOWLY)

430
00:29:38,568 --> 00:29:39,911
(TYPING SLOWLY)

431
00:29:40,362 --> 00:29:41,363
(BELL RINGS)

432
00:29:47,703 --> 00:29:49,296
(TYPING EXTREMELY SLOW)

433
00:29:51,331 --> 00:29:52,753
(TYPING SLOWLY)

434
00:29:54,126 --> 00:29:55,878
(TYPING NORMALLY)

435
00:29:56,795 --> 00:29:58,263
(BARELY TYPING)

436
00:30:04,052 --> 00:30:06,100
(TYPING QUICKLY)

437
00:30:15,772 --> 00:30:17,774
You need a secretary. Pick one.

438
00:30:19,735 --> 00:30:22,989
I don't know how. They're all so qualified.

439
00:30:24,239 --> 00:30:26,412
You have to choose.

440
00:30:27,659 --> 00:30:28,660
(SIGHS)

441
00:30:29,286 --> 00:30:31,846
PHOTOGRAPHER: - Prosze sie ladnie usmiechac.
- Big smile. Big smile.

442
00:30:31,913 --> 00:30:32,914
Uwaga.

443
00:30:33,582 --> 00:30:34,583
(CAMERA SHUTTER CLICKS)

444
00:30:37,711 --> 00:30:40,089
(PLAYING STATELY MUSIC ON PIANO)

445
00:30:53,018 --> 00:30:55,862
If you would, look after my guest.

446
00:30:57,439 --> 00:31:00,659
SCHINDLER: Boxed teas are good. Coffee, pâté,

447
00:31:01,777 --> 00:31:04,621
kielbasa sausage, cheeses, Beluga caviar.

448
00:31:05,030 --> 00:31:06,373
- Yes.
- And, of course,

449
00:31:06,448 --> 00:31:07,540
who could live without German cigarettes?

450
00:31:07,616 --> 00:31:09,243
Get me as many as you can find.

451
00:31:10,118 --> 00:31:11,410
And some more fresh fruit.

452
00:31:12,204 --> 00:31:14,627
The real rarities, oranges, lemons, pineapples.

453
00:31:15,290 --> 00:31:17,713
- I need several boxes of Cuban cigars. The best.
- Yes.

454
00:31:17,792 --> 00:31:19,636
And dark, unsweetened chocolate.

455
00:31:19,711 --> 00:31:21,634
Not in the shape of ladyfingers, the chunk chocolate.

456
00:31:21,713 --> 00:31:23,715
Big as my hand, you sample at wine tastings.

457
00:31:23,799 --> 00:31:24,800
Yes.

458
00:31:24,925 --> 00:31:28,145
We're going to need lots of cognac. The best, Hennessy.

459
00:31:28,220 --> 00:31:32,521
Dom Pérignon champagne. Get L'Espadon sardines,

460
00:31:33,058 --> 00:31:36,232
and, oh, try to find nylon stockings.

461
00:31:39,773 --> 00:31:42,617
SCHINDLER: It is my distinct pleasure to announce

462
00:31:42,692 --> 00:31:48,211
the fully operational status of Deutsche Emailwarenfabrik.

463
00:31:48,211 --> 00:31:49,074
FOREMAN: Patrz jakies szmaty!

464
00:31:49,074 --> 00:31:52,795
Manufacturers of superior enamelware crockery,

465
00:31:53,703 --> 00:31:57,753
expressly designed and crafted for military use.

466
00:31:58,834 --> 00:32:01,383
Utilizing only the most modern equipment,

467
00:32:01,461 --> 00:32:03,784
D.E.F.'s staff of highly skilled

468
00:32:03,784 --> 00:32:06,308
and experienced artisans and journeymen

469
00:32:06,383 --> 00:32:09,353
deliver a product of unparalleled quality,

470
00:32:10,262 --> 00:32:14,233
enabling me to proffer,
with absolute confidence and pride,

471
00:32:14,307 --> 00:32:16,776
a full line of field and kitchenware

472
00:32:16,852 --> 00:32:19,856
unsurpassable in all respects by my competitors.

473
00:32:21,022 --> 00:32:23,666
See attached list and available colors.

474
00:32:25,193 --> 00:32:28,197
Anticipating the enclosed bids
will meet with your approval

475
00:32:28,780 --> 00:32:32,330
and looking forward to a long and
mutually prosperous association,

476
00:32:32,742 --> 00:32:35,916
I extend to you in advance my sincerest gratitude

477
00:32:35,996 --> 00:32:38,875
and very best regards. Oskar Schindler.

478
00:32:38,957 --> 00:32:42,257
Yeah, I need 700 gross from here for next Thursday.

479
00:32:42,335 --> 00:32:44,963
Nine hundred, no, make it 10 for Wednesday.

480
00:32:45,046 --> 00:32:47,189
All this stuff here goes to Madritsch's factory

481
00:32:47,189 --> 00:32:48,850
on Tuesday of next week.

482
00:32:57,100 --> 00:32:58,522
(HORN HONKING)

483
00:33:05,192 --> 00:33:08,412
SCHINDLER: My father was fond of saying,
"You need three things in life,

484
00:33:08,487 --> 00:33:09,790
a good doctor,

485
00:33:10,571 --> 00:33:12,674
a forgiving priest,

486
00:33:13,257 --> 00:33:14,718
and a clever accountant."

487
00:33:19,331 --> 00:33:23,131
The first two, I've never had much use for.

488
00:33:23,585 --> 00:33:25,553
But the third...

489
00:33:32,594 --> 00:33:35,222
Just pretend, for Christ's sake.

490
00:33:39,768 --> 00:33:41,645
Is that all?

491
00:33:47,692 --> 00:33:49,071
I'm trying to thank you.

492
00:33:51,095 --> 00:33:53,819
I'm saying I couldn't have done this without you.

493
00:33:57,827 --> 00:34:00,376
The usual thing would be to acknowledge my gratitude.

494
00:34:00,455 --> 00:34:02,958
It would also, by the way, be the courteous thing.

495
00:34:05,627 --> 00:34:06,775
You're welcome.

496
00:34:10,465 --> 00:34:11,466
(SIGHS)

497
00:34:17,088 --> 00:34:18,140
Get out of here.

498
00:34:23,728 --> 00:34:24,729
(DOOR BUZZES)

499
00:34:32,612 --> 00:34:34,559
SCHINDLER:
Klonowska, who is it?

500
00:34:45,292 --> 00:34:47,711
She's so embarrassed. Look at her.

501
00:35:00,181 --> 00:35:01,683
You know something? You would like her.

502
00:35:02,267 --> 00:35:03,769
- Oskar, please.
- What?

503
00:35:04,394 --> 00:35:06,613
- I don't have to like her just because you do.
- You would, though.

504
00:35:06,688 --> 00:35:08,016
It doesn't work that way.

505
00:35:12,277 --> 00:35:13,278
(DOOR CLOSES)

506
00:35:22,746 --> 00:35:24,048
You've done well here.

507
00:35:27,375 --> 00:35:29,019
You look wonderful.

508
00:35:33,006 --> 00:35:34,599
Be careful of the step, miss.

509
00:35:34,716 --> 00:35:36,343
Mrs. Schindler, Marek.

510
00:35:41,389 --> 00:35:43,687
(GLOOMY SUNDAY PLAYING)

511
00:35:49,731 --> 00:35:51,153
It's not a charade, all this?

512
00:35:51,674 --> 00:35:53,876
- How could it be a charade?
- The clothes,

513
00:35:53,935 --> 00:35:55,612
- the car, the apartment.
- Wait a minute.

514
00:35:55,695 --> 00:35:58,574
Take a guess how many people are on my payroll.

515
00:35:58,657 --> 00:35:59,783
Oskar.

516
00:36:00,116 --> 00:36:04,862
My father, at the height of his success,
had 50. I've got 350.

517
00:36:05,337 --> 00:36:06,756
Three hundred and fifty workers

518
00:36:06,873 --> 00:36:09,092
on the factory floor, with one purpose.

519
00:36:10,335 --> 00:36:11,962
To make pots and pans?

520
00:36:12,045 --> 00:36:14,969
To make money. For me.

521
00:36:16,299 --> 00:36:17,300
(SIGHS)

522
00:36:22,138 --> 00:36:24,732
Does anyone ask about me?

523
00:36:26,768 --> 00:36:27,663
Back home?

524
00:36:28,686 --> 00:36:29,747
Everybody.

525
00:36:29,938 --> 00:36:31,111
All the time.

526
00:36:32,982 --> 00:36:33,983
Hmm.

527
00:36:37,153 --> 00:36:41,454
They won't soon forget the name
Schindler here, I can tell you that.

528
00:36:43,451 --> 00:36:45,795
"Oskar Schindler," they'll say.

529
00:36:46,579 --> 00:36:48,128
"Everybody remembers him."

530
00:36:50,667 --> 00:36:53,796
"He did something extraordinary."

531
00:36:55,463 --> 00:36:57,607
"He did something no one else did."

532
00:37:00,051 --> 00:37:02,050
"He came here with nothing,

533
00:37:03,430 --> 00:37:04,979
a suitcase,

534
00:37:06,141 --> 00:37:10,442
and built a bankrupt company into a major manufactory."

535
00:37:12,564 --> 00:37:15,238
"And left with a steamer trunk."

536
00:37:15,608 --> 00:37:18,657
"Two steamer trunks full of money."

537
00:37:20,488 --> 00:37:22,832
"All the riches of the world."

538
00:37:24,951 --> 00:37:26,203
Hmm.

539
00:37:26,870 --> 00:37:29,794
It's comforting to see that nothing's changed.

540
00:37:30,073 --> 00:37:31,692
You're wrong, Emilie.

541
00:37:34,085 --> 00:37:36,759
There's no way I could have known this before,

542
00:37:36,838 --> 00:37:38,682
but there was always something missing.

543
00:37:39,883 --> 00:37:43,808
In every business I tried,
I can see now, it wasn't me that failed.

544
00:37:43,887 --> 00:37:45,640
Something was missing.

545
00:37:46,806 --> 00:37:50,276
Even if I'd known what it was,
there's nothing I could have done about it,

546
00:37:50,351 --> 00:37:53,730
because you can't create this thing.

547
00:37:55,190 --> 00:37:59,036
And it makes all the difference
in the world between success and failure.

548
00:38:01,029 --> 00:38:02,497
Luck?

549
00:38:05,200 --> 00:38:06,543
War.

550
00:38:09,204 --> 00:38:11,332
(BALLROOM DANCE MUSIC PLAYING)

551
00:38:31,643 --> 00:38:33,395
Should I stay?

552
00:38:36,231 --> 00:38:38,025
It's a beautiful city.

553
00:38:40,568 --> 00:38:42,412
I asked you if I should stay.

554
00:38:45,740 --> 00:38:47,413
It's up to you.

555
00:38:52,664 --> 00:38:53,965
Promise me, Oskar,

556
00:38:54,290 --> 00:38:56,926
no doorman or maitre d' will ever presume

557
00:38:57,043 --> 00:38:59,098
I'm anyone other than Mrs. Schindler,

558
00:38:59,654 --> 00:39:00,890
and I'll stay.

559
00:39:04,425 --> 00:39:05,927
Goodbye, darling.

560
00:39:08,596 --> 00:39:09,813
(SPEAKING INDISTINCTLY)

561
00:39:23,444 --> 00:39:27,049
I could try to read this,
or I could eat my lunch while it's still hot.

562
00:39:28,374 --> 00:39:29,451
We're doing well?

563
00:39:29,601 --> 00:39:30,998
- Yes.
- Better this month than last?

564
00:39:30,998 --> 00:39:31,520
Yes.

565
00:39:31,520 --> 00:39:33,342
Any reason to think next month will be worse?

566
00:39:33,421 --> 00:39:34,648
The war could end.

567
00:39:46,968 --> 00:39:47,569
What?

568
00:39:47,736 --> 00:39:49,157
There is a machinist outside

569
00:39:49,162 --> 00:39:51,732
who'd like to thank you personally for giving him a job.

570
00:39:52,807 --> 00:39:56,282
Every day he comes. He's very grateful.
It'll just take a minute.

571
00:40:03,526 --> 00:40:05,028
Mr. Lowenstein?

572
00:40:12,994 --> 00:40:15,626
I want to thank you, sir,

573
00:40:15,839 --> 00:40:18,706
for giving me the opportunity to work.

574
00:40:18,708 --> 00:40:20,711
You're welcome. I'm sure you're doing a great job.

575
00:40:21,878 --> 00:40:23,549
The SS beat me up.

576
00:40:23,771 --> 00:40:25,849
They would have killed me,

577
00:40:26,641 --> 00:40:30,767
but I am essential to the war effort, thanks to you.

578
00:40:30,845 --> 00:40:32,038
That's great.

579
00:40:32,972 --> 00:40:34,849
I work hard for you.

580
00:40:35,516 --> 00:40:36,414
I'm sure you do.

581
00:40:36,459 --> 00:40:39,278
I'll continue to work hard for you.

582
00:40:40,146 --> 00:40:41,568
That's great. Thanks.

583
00:40:41,689 --> 00:40:42,781
God bless you, sir.

584
00:40:43,499 --> 00:40:44,225
All right.

585
00:40:44,275 --> 00:40:46,152
You are a good man.

586
00:40:49,906 --> 00:40:50,898
He saved my life.

587
00:40:50,898 --> 00:40:51,707
Yes, he did.

588
00:40:52,909 --> 00:40:53,800
God bless him.

589
00:40:53,802 --> 00:40:55,528
Yeah. Come on.

590
00:40:57,997 --> 00:40:59,545
God bless you.

591
00:41:12,553 --> 00:41:15,272
STERN: I'm sorry, Herr Direktor, but you're running very late.

592
00:41:15,348 --> 00:41:18,426
This is for the Obersturmbannführer, sir.

593
00:41:18,426 --> 00:41:21,446
And this is for his niece, Greta. It's her birthday.

594
00:41:21,521 --> 00:41:25,324
- Greta as in Garbo.
- By the way, don't ever do that to me again.

595
00:41:25,566 --> 00:41:27,159
Go, go, drive! Don't bow.

596
00:41:27,235 --> 00:41:29,235
Did you happen to notice that that man had one arm?

597
00:41:29,237 --> 00:41:30,238
Did he?

598
00:41:34,909 --> 00:41:36,001
What's his use?

599
00:41:36,119 --> 00:41:37,045
Very useful.

600
00:41:40,373 --> 00:41:40,949
How?

601
00:41:40,949 --> 00:41:43,001
Very useful! Success!

602
00:41:51,259 --> 00:41:53,637
Achrar kach loe nedah...

603
00:42:12,822 --> 00:42:14,720
OFFICER: Besen und Schaufeln werden ausgeladen

604
00:42:14,841 --> 00:42:17,482
jeder nimmt sich eine und dann
wird die Straße gereinigt.

605
00:42:17,827 --> 00:42:19,704
We'll be late for work.

606
00:42:19,787 --> 00:42:21,289
(HORN HONKING)

607
00:42:32,550 --> 00:42:35,053
CZURDA: You shouldn't think of them as yours, Oskar.

608
00:42:35,386 --> 00:42:37,480
You need to understand that some of the officers here

609
00:42:37,555 --> 00:42:39,478
don't give a damn about production.

610
00:42:40,641 --> 00:42:44,360
To them, it's a matter of national priority

611
00:42:44,478 --> 00:42:46,197
that Jews be made to shovel snow.

612
00:42:47,148 --> 00:42:50,994
It's got nothing to do with reality, Oskar.
You know it and I know it.

613
00:42:51,944 --> 00:42:57,216
Jews shoveling snow, it's got a ritual significance.

614
00:43:00,536 --> 00:43:01,509
Come with me.

615
00:43:01,830 --> 00:43:04,003
I am an essential worker.

616
00:43:04,082 --> 00:43:05,083
Essential worker?
(LAUGHS)

617
00:43:05,208 --> 00:43:06,255
Yes.

618
00:43:07,251 --> 00:43:08,423
(OFFICER LAUGHING)

619
00:43:09,670 --> 00:43:11,513
I work for Oskar Schindler.

620
00:43:11,672 --> 00:43:13,515
Essential worker for Oskar Schindler.

621
00:43:13,591 --> 00:43:14,912
- A one-armed Jew?
- Yes, yes.

622
00:43:15,009 --> 00:43:16,352
Twice as useless!

623
00:43:17,720 --> 00:43:20,039
I lost a day of production, Rolf.

624
00:43:20,681 --> 00:43:22,934
LOWENSTEIN:
I work for Oskar Schindler.

625
00:43:24,519 --> 00:43:27,464
Danka, look at the snow.
Look at the snow. Look at the snow!

626
00:43:28,356 --> 00:43:29,357
(GUNSHOT)

627
00:43:32,360 --> 00:43:35,864
I lost a worker. I expect to be compensated.

628
00:43:35,947 --> 00:43:39,042
File a grievance with the Economic Office. It's your right.

629
00:43:39,117 --> 00:43:40,209
Would it do any good?

630
00:43:40,668 --> 00:43:42,370
Of course not.
(CHUCKLES)

631
00:43:44,872 --> 00:43:48,372
A big shot from the SS Budget
and Construction Office came to lunch,

632
00:43:48,451 --> 00:43:52,802
and he told us that to believe
the Jewish skilled worker had a place

633
00:43:52,880 --> 00:43:56,134
in Reich economics was a treasonable idea.

634
00:44:01,389 --> 00:44:05,360
A one-armed machinist, Oskar?

635
00:44:05,852 --> 00:44:07,120
He was a metal press operator.

636
00:44:12,733 --> 00:44:14,076
Quite skilled.

637
00:44:16,696 --> 00:44:18,448
(MOANING)

638
00:44:26,873 --> 00:44:28,591
(PANTING AND MOANING)

639
00:44:44,265 --> 00:44:45,437
Herr Direktor?

640
00:44:46,267 --> 00:44:48,395
Shit. I don't believe it.

641
00:44:48,561 --> 00:44:50,189
Stern, is that you?

642
00:44:51,105 --> 00:44:54,450
No, it's Poldek. It's about Stern.

643
00:44:55,276 --> 00:44:58,280
SOLDIER ON PA: Leave your luggage on the platform.

644
00:44:59,197 --> 00:45:04,499
Clearly label it. Given name first, then your surname.

645
00:45:05,244 --> 00:45:07,622
Do not bring your baggage with you.

646
00:45:07,872 --> 00:45:10,250
It will follow you later.

647
00:45:11,083 --> 00:45:16,335
Leave your luggage on the platform. Clearly label it.

648
00:45:16,455 --> 00:45:19,304
MAN ON LOUDSPEAKER: Lassen Sie Ihr Gepäck am Bahnsteig.

649
00:45:21,302 --> 00:45:22,279
Stern?

650
00:45:25,556 --> 00:45:26,682
He's on the list.

651
00:45:27,007 --> 00:45:27,879
He is?

652
00:45:28,851 --> 00:45:30,148
Well, let's find him.

653
00:45:30,269 --> 00:45:31,521
I'm sorry. You can't have him.

654
00:45:31,604 --> 00:45:34,904
He's on the list. If he were
an essential worker he would not be on the list.

655
00:45:34,982 --> 00:45:36,855
I'm talking to a clerk. What is your name?

656
00:45:37,401 --> 00:45:40,454
- Sir, the list is correct.
- I didn't ask you about the list.

657
00:45:40,529 --> 00:45:41,501
I asked you your name.

658
00:45:42,122 --> 00:45:43,194
Klaus Tauber.

659
00:45:45,326 --> 00:45:46,223
Tauber.

660
00:45:46,702 --> 00:45:50,707
Hauptscharführer, this gentleman
thinks a mistake's been made.

661
00:45:50,790 --> 00:45:53,134
My plant manager is somewhere on this train.

662
00:45:53,668 --> 00:45:55,591
If it leaves with him on it, it'll disrupt production

663
00:45:55,670 --> 00:45:57,672
and the Armaments Board will want to know why.

664
00:45:57,755 --> 00:45:59,382
MAN ON LOUDSPEAKER: Achtung, dieser Zug fährt jetzt ab.

665
00:45:59,465 --> 00:46:00,438
Is he on the list?

666
00:46:00,459 --> 00:46:01,432
Yes, sir.

667
00:46:02,459 --> 00:46:03,932
Itzhak Stern.

668
00:46:04,929 --> 00:46:09,555
Well, the list is correct, sir. There is nothing I can do.

669
00:46:10,851 --> 00:46:11,770
What is your name?

670
00:46:11,770 --> 00:46:13,567
SOLDIER ON PA:
Close and secure all doors.

671
00:46:13,646 --> 00:46:16,991
- My name? My name is Kunder.
- This train is now leaving.

672
00:46:17,483 --> 00:46:19,110
- Kunder.
- Hauptscharführer Kunder.

673
00:46:19,193 --> 00:46:21,116
- K-U-N-D-E-R.
- And what's yours?

674
00:46:21,195 --> 00:46:23,493
Schindler. S-C-H-I-N-D-L-E-R.

675
00:46:23,948 --> 00:46:26,121
Gentlemen, thank you very much.

676
00:46:26,200 --> 00:46:28,669
I think I can guarantee you you'll both be

677
00:46:28,744 --> 00:46:31,064
in Southern Russia before the end of the month.

678
00:46:31,339 --> 00:46:32,023
Good day.

679
00:46:32,039 --> 00:46:34,399
CONDUCTOR: Zamykac dokladnie drzwi. Dokladnie zamykac drzwi.

680
00:46:35,835 --> 00:46:37,428
Stern!

681
00:46:38,254 --> 00:46:40,097
Stern! Stern!

682
00:46:40,172 --> 00:46:41,549
Itzhak Stern!

683
00:46:41,632 --> 00:46:43,225
(TRAIN WHISTLE BLOWING)

684
00:46:48,806 --> 00:46:50,558
Stern! Itzhak Stern!

685
00:46:50,766 --> 00:46:52,063
KUNDER: Stern! Stern!

686
00:46:52,184 --> 00:46:53,481
Itzhak Stern!

687
00:46:55,062 --> 00:46:56,154
Stern!

688
00:46:56,230 --> 00:46:57,402
Sir?

689
00:47:00,526 --> 00:47:02,528
- Stern!
- Herr Direktor.

690
00:47:02,611 --> 00:47:03,828
(COUGHING)

691
00:47:03,904 --> 00:47:04,897
My apologies.

692
00:47:05,906 --> 00:47:07,123
Stop the train!

693
00:47:07,241 --> 00:47:08,493
- I apologize.
- He's here!

694
00:47:08,784 --> 00:47:09,752
Stop the train!

695
00:47:10,870 --> 00:47:12,793
Stop the train! Stop it!

696
00:47:25,760 --> 00:47:28,104
Sign here. Initial there.

697
00:47:28,220 --> 00:47:30,222
(WHISTLE BLOWING)

698
00:47:30,306 --> 00:47:34,277
Makes no difference to us,
you understand. This one, that one.

699
00:47:34,935 --> 00:47:37,029
(TRAIN WHISTLE BLOWING)
 It's the inconvenience to the list.

700
00:47:37,104 --> 00:47:38,022
It's the paperwork.

701
00:47:38,239 --> 00:47:40,691
I somehow left my work card at home.

702
00:47:40,775 --> 00:47:44,200
I tried to explain them it was a mistake, but...

703
00:47:44,820 --> 00:47:46,008
I'm sorry, it was stupid!

704
00:47:46,106 --> 00:47:48,029
What if I got here five minutes later?

705
00:47:48,908 --> 00:47:50,411
Then where would I be?

706
00:49:33,387 --> 00:49:36,140
Sacrean, sacrean end tzukeer.

707
00:49:36,474 --> 00:49:38,977
Tzukeer end sacrean.

708
00:49:42,938 --> 00:49:45,487
I woke up from a dream this morning.

709
00:49:45,566 --> 00:49:49,662
I was broke and sharing a room
with 12 people I didn't know,

710
00:49:49,737 --> 00:49:52,456
only to discover I was broke

711
00:49:52,573 --> 00:49:55,326
and sharing a room with 12 people I didn't know.

712
00:49:55,409 --> 00:49:57,036
(CHUCKLING)
You laugh about it?

713
00:49:57,119 --> 00:49:58,371
I have to laugh.

714
00:49:58,913 --> 00:50:01,166
You are living behind walls.

715
00:50:01,248 --> 00:50:06,175
The walls I can deal with.
It's the restrictions to my life I'm tired of.

716
00:50:06,253 --> 00:50:09,348
Those walls keep them out. That's all I care about.

717
00:50:09,423 --> 00:50:12,267
I like it here. There's a kind of, I don't know,

718
00:50:12,343 --> 00:50:14,721
ancestral squalor to it all.

719
00:50:14,929 --> 00:50:16,931
You are a slave to these people. No one envies us anymore.

720
00:50:17,014 --> 00:50:18,015
I'm smart.

721
00:50:18,140 --> 00:50:19,187
Oh, yes, you're smart.

722
00:50:19,266 --> 00:50:20,267
You're a real genius.

723
00:50:20,434 --> 00:50:21,481
You had your chance.

724
00:50:21,769 --> 00:50:25,945
Today, today I actually found time to organize a thought.

725
00:50:26,774 --> 00:50:29,448
I can't remember the last time I did that.

726
00:50:29,527 --> 00:50:31,279
When's the last time we did this?

727
00:50:31,362 --> 00:50:33,581
When's the last time we stood around and talked?

728
00:50:33,822 --> 00:50:36,450
No one ordered me onto a truck today.

729
00:50:37,075 --> 00:50:39,203
No one took my business away from me.

730
00:50:39,578 --> 00:50:41,421
You don't really have a business to take.

731
00:50:41,914 --> 00:50:43,791
There's nowhere down from here.

732
00:50:43,958 --> 00:50:45,835
This is it. This is the bottom.

733
00:50:46,585 --> 00:50:49,134
The ghetto is liberty.

734
00:50:56,679 --> 00:50:59,649
This street divides the ghetto just about in half.

735
00:51:00,599 --> 00:51:02,283
Right side, Ghetto A,

736
00:51:03,499 --> 00:51:07,483
civil employees, industrial workers, and so on.

737
00:51:08,757 --> 00:51:10,677
Left side, Ghetto B,

738
00:51:11,657 --> 00:51:12,777
surplus labor,

739
00:51:13,694 --> 00:51:15,848
the elderly and infirm, mostly.

740
00:51:16,282 --> 00:51:18,501
Which is where you will want to start.

741
00:51:18,659 --> 00:51:20,912
Do you have any questions, sir?

742
00:51:21,161 --> 00:51:23,789
Ja, why is the top down? I'm fucking freezing.

743
00:51:26,584 --> 00:51:29,133
KUNDE: We expect a labor force of 25,000 to 30,000.

744
00:51:29,211 --> 00:51:32,761
Segregated, of course.
The men in the barracks near the quarry.

745
00:51:32,840 --> 00:51:34,308
The women on the other side of the wire.

746
00:51:34,383 --> 00:51:36,477
- GOETH: My place is where?
- There, sir.

747
00:51:36,552 --> 00:51:38,350
- There?
- Yes, the villa.

748
00:51:38,429 --> 00:51:39,851
You call that a villa?

749
00:51:39,930 --> 00:51:41,170
Their synagogue, can you see it?

750
00:51:41,181 --> 00:51:42,307
That's not a villa.

751
00:51:42,391 --> 00:51:44,064
We're planning to turn it into the camp stables.

752
00:51:44,143 --> 00:51:45,144
It's a house.

753
00:51:45,769 --> 00:51:47,612
GOETH: What's that over there?

754
00:51:50,065 --> 00:51:51,988
KUNDE: Kindergarten. Fifty-one.

755
00:51:52,467 --> 00:51:56,573
And we have a dentist, shoemaker, practical physicians.

756
00:51:59,783 --> 00:52:01,535
There they are, sir.

757
00:52:03,078 --> 00:52:05,080
(SNIFFS) Hmm.

758
00:52:07,041 --> 00:52:09,861
One of you is a very lucky girl.

759
00:52:11,045 --> 00:52:15,721
There is an opening for a job away from all this

760
00:52:16,508 --> 00:52:20,012
backbreaking work at my new villa.

761
00:52:20,554 --> 00:52:21,555
(SNIFFS)

762
00:52:22,264 --> 00:52:23,265
Uh...

763
00:52:25,976 --> 00:52:29,071
Which of you has domestic experience?

764
00:52:33,067 --> 00:52:36,788
Ja, on second thought,
I don't really want someone else's maid.

765
00:52:36,862 --> 00:52:40,082
All those annoying habits I have to undo.

766
00:52:45,245 --> 00:52:47,519
(CHUCKLES) I don't want to give you my cold.

767
00:52:48,666 --> 00:52:50,088
What's your name?

768
00:52:50,876 --> 00:52:52,594
- Helen Hirsch.
- What?

769
00:52:53,045 --> 00:52:54,968
Helen Hirsch.
(COUGHING)

770
00:52:55,047 --> 00:52:56,845
What? I can't hear.

771
00:52:56,965 --> 00:52:58,763
Helen Hirsch.

772
00:53:14,942 --> 00:53:15,943
Ja.

773
00:53:16,026 --> 00:53:17,369
WOMAN: Take it all down!

774
00:53:17,444 --> 00:53:19,572
(OFFICER BLOWS WHISTLE)
OFFICER: To work!

775
00:53:21,657 --> 00:53:24,376
WOMAN: Take it down! It is not safe!

776
00:53:24,451 --> 00:53:27,500
She says the foundation was poured wrong.
She's got to take it down.

777
00:53:27,579 --> 00:53:30,549
I told her it's the barracks, not the fucking Hotel Europa.

778
00:53:30,624 --> 00:53:34,424
Fucking Jew bitch engineer. You fucking bitch!

779
00:53:34,962 --> 00:53:38,932
Herr Kommandant. The entire foundation

780
00:53:39,149 --> 00:53:41,974
has to be torn down and re-poured.

781
00:53:42,052 --> 00:53:45,571
If not, there will be at least a subsidence

782
00:53:45,688 --> 00:53:47,607
at the southern end of the barracks.

783
00:53:48,267 --> 00:53:50,986
Subsidence, and then collapse.

784
00:53:51,061 --> 00:53:53,063
- And you are an engineer?
- Yes.

785
00:53:53,897 --> 00:53:55,319
My name is Diana Reiter.

786
00:53:55,399 --> 00:53:58,824
I'm a graduate of civil engineering
from the University of Milan.

787
00:53:58,902 --> 00:54:02,497
An educated Jew, like Karl Marx himself.

788
00:54:10,831 --> 00:54:12,629
- Unterscharführer!
- Jawohl?

789
00:54:14,460 --> 00:54:15,586
Shoot her.

790
00:54:15,711 --> 00:54:16,883
What?

791
00:54:17,212 --> 00:54:18,213
(GASPS)

792
00:54:18,672 --> 00:54:22,927
Herr Kommandant, I'm only trying to do my job.

793
00:54:23,010 --> 00:54:24,557
Ja, I'm doing mine.

794
00:54:24,636 --> 00:54:26,980
But, sir, she's foreman of construction.

795
00:54:27,055 --> 00:54:29,774
We are not going to have arguments with these people.

796
00:54:29,850 --> 00:54:30,976
Okay.

797
00:54:33,187 --> 00:54:36,612
No. Shoot her here, on my authority.

798
00:54:47,785 --> 00:54:49,628
It will take more than that.

799
00:54:49,703 --> 00:54:51,046
GOETH: I'm sure you're right.

800
00:54:59,296 --> 00:55:03,642
Take it down, re-pour it, rebuild it. Like she said.

801
00:55:04,384 --> 00:55:07,934
We have more to see,
but we have only one more hour of light.

802
00:55:11,058 --> 00:55:12,560
(HUMMING)

803
00:55:19,566 --> 00:55:21,443
GOETH: Today is history.

804
00:55:23,320 --> 00:55:25,914
Today will be remembered.

805
00:55:27,741 --> 00:55:33,373
Years from now, the young will ask
with wonder about this day.

806
00:55:35,415 --> 00:55:37,793
Today is history, and you are part of it.

807
00:55:40,796 --> 00:55:43,140
Six hundred years ago,

808
00:55:43,257 --> 00:55:46,226
(SINGING MORNING PRAYER)
when elsewhere they were footing
the blame for the Black Death,

809
00:55:47,154 --> 00:55:50,806
Kazimierz the Great... Ke laolam chasedo

810
00:55:50,889 --> 00:55:54,190
...so-called, told the Jews they could come to Krakow.

811
00:55:55,269 --> 00:55:56,236
They came.

812
00:55:56,411 --> 00:55:57,530
Not that fat.

813
00:55:57,530 --> 00:56:00,028
- They trundled their belongings into the city.
- No, less.

814
00:56:00,107 --> 00:56:01,275
Yes, a bit less.

815
00:56:01,275 --> 00:56:02,322
They settled.

816
00:56:03,277 --> 00:56:05,405
They took hold.

817
00:56:05,487 --> 00:56:11,039
They prospered in business, science, education, the arts.

818
00:56:12,953 --> 00:56:14,705
They came here with nothing.

819
00:56:15,122 --> 00:56:16,123
(PRAYING CONTINUES)

820
00:56:16,748 --> 00:56:17,965
Nothing.

821
00:56:19,418 --> 00:56:20,761
And they flourished.

822
00:56:22,296 --> 00:56:26,847
For six centuries there has been a Jewish Krakow.

823
00:56:28,427 --> 00:56:29,804
Think about that.

824
00:56:30,679 --> 00:56:32,272
By this evening,

825
00:56:33,015 --> 00:56:35,894
those six centuries are a rumor.

826
00:56:38,228 --> 00:56:39,525
They never happened.

827
00:56:41,648 --> 00:56:43,321
Today is history.

828
00:57:04,546 --> 00:57:08,174
Absitzen und in Zweierreihen antreten!

829
00:57:08,675 --> 00:57:10,427
Kameraden! Schneller!

830
00:57:10,510 --> 00:57:12,103
(WHISTLE BLOWING)
(INDISTINCT SHOUTING)

831
00:57:14,181 --> 00:57:18,152
I think we will start with Ghetto B.

832
00:57:18,226 --> 00:57:20,069
SOLDIERS: Hopp, hopp, hopp, hopp!

833
00:57:22,147 --> 00:57:23,649
(DOGS BARKING)

834
00:57:28,028 --> 00:57:29,120
All right?

835
00:57:29,237 --> 00:57:30,329
Start from both sides?

836
00:57:30,405 --> 00:57:32,282
- No, no, I would like you to start there.
- The right side?

837
00:57:32,366 --> 00:57:34,538
The right side, yeah. And then move around.

838
00:57:34,600 --> 00:57:35,472
All right.

839
00:57:37,037 --> 00:57:41,543
MAN ON LOUDSPEAKER:
Beschriften Sie es deutlich. Es wird Ihnen nachgeschickt.

840
00:57:42,918 --> 00:57:45,137
Lassen Sie Ihr Gepäck stehen.

841
00:57:47,631 --> 00:57:50,180
Beschriften Sie es deutlich.

842
00:57:50,342 --> 00:57:53,186
Es wird Ihnen nachgeschickt.

843
00:57:54,304 --> 00:57:55,806
SOLDIER: Aufmachen!

844
00:57:56,223 --> 00:57:57,224
Lakoom.

845
00:57:57,516 --> 00:58:00,556
Loe ani loe yodahat olay kaday lehachnese
at hayladeem mitachat la shoolchan.

846
00:58:00,727 --> 00:58:03,025
Mitacht la meeta. Bohow.

847
00:58:07,985 --> 00:58:10,079
SOLDIERS: Hopp, hopp, hopp, hopp!

848
00:58:10,404 --> 00:58:12,156
(SOLDIERS SHOUTING IN GERMAN)

849
00:58:52,112 --> 00:58:53,955
SOLDIERS: Runterkommen!

850
00:58:55,824 --> 00:58:58,327
(SOLDIERS BANGING ON DOORS)

851
00:59:01,663 --> 00:59:02,880
SOLDIER: Rauskommen, Juden!

852
00:59:08,336 --> 00:59:09,758
SOLDIER: Na platz!

853
00:59:10,130 --> 00:59:11,131
(WHISTLE BLOWING)

854
00:59:19,389 --> 00:59:20,811
Gold, Chaim!

855
00:59:20,932 --> 00:59:22,400
Platz Chodi.

856
00:59:23,143 --> 00:59:24,315
Gold, Chaim!

857
00:59:24,478 --> 00:59:25,695
Yes, Chaim Gold.

858
00:59:25,771 --> 00:59:27,398
Shoot him.

859
00:59:27,481 --> 00:59:30,610
(SPEAKING POLISH)

860
00:59:33,612 --> 00:59:36,115
Nie zabijaj! Nie zabijaj!

861
00:59:36,573 --> 00:59:37,825
(WOMAN SCREAMS)

862
00:59:37,908 --> 00:59:40,708
SOLDIER: Ziehen Sie warme Kleidung
an und begeben Sie sich zum Platz...

863
00:59:41,286 --> 00:59:44,005
Keine Koffer! Keine Koffer!

864
00:59:44,164 --> 00:59:45,381
Gehen Sie durch!

865
00:59:45,499 --> 00:59:47,843
Keine Koffer! Keine Koffer!

866
00:59:48,251 --> 00:59:50,470
Hey, little boy. Wie alt bist du, he?

867
00:59:50,545 --> 00:59:53,674
Na, wie heißt du? Wie heißt du, eh?

868
00:59:54,549 --> 00:59:56,472
Keine Koffer, hab ich gesagt!

869
00:59:56,551 --> 00:59:57,677
(SCREAMING)

870
00:59:57,761 --> 00:59:59,604
Packt keine Koffer! Keine Koffer!

871
01:00:00,430 --> 01:00:02,899
Rüber zum Platz Chodi! Koffer hier lassen!

872
01:00:02,974 --> 01:00:05,648
Blauschein. Platz Chodi!

873
01:00:06,186 --> 01:00:07,859
Was soll der Koffer hier?

874
01:00:08,021 --> 01:00:10,194
Alles rüber zum Platz Chodi!

875
01:00:13,151 --> 01:00:15,153
(SOLDIERS CONTINUE SHOUTING)

876
01:00:23,870 --> 01:00:25,838
(RUNNING FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING)

877
01:00:27,690 --> 01:00:29,008
We're getting out through the sewers.

878
01:00:29,084 --> 01:00:30,802
I have to check if they're clear.

879
01:00:32,379 --> 01:00:34,427
Mila, pack some things. Nothing bigger than this.

880
01:00:34,506 --> 01:00:37,510
I can't go in the sewers. I told you I won't go in the sewers.

881
01:00:41,596 --> 01:00:44,395
You can go. Yes, you can.

882
01:00:47,394 --> 01:00:49,021
I won't go in the sewers.

883
01:00:51,690 --> 01:00:53,033
(WHISTLE BLOWING)

884
01:01:00,574 --> 01:01:03,874
SOLDIER: Move! Over there! Over there! Move!

885
01:01:04,578 --> 01:01:06,151
Your card, Jew!

886
01:01:06,913 --> 01:01:08,634
Define Papiere, Jude!

887
01:01:09,417 --> 01:01:11,361
Define Papiere, Jude!

888
01:01:13,587 --> 01:01:14,634
That line.

889
01:01:16,089 --> 01:01:17,636
Deine Karte!

890
01:01:17,757 --> 01:01:20,761
Ich geh nicht rauf wegen Deiner verdammten Karte!

891
01:01:22,596 --> 01:01:24,143
Deine Scheißkarte!

892
01:01:30,937 --> 01:01:32,530
(WHISTLE BLOWING)

893
01:01:34,649 --> 01:01:36,447
(WOMAN WAILING IN GERMAN)

894
01:01:43,617 --> 01:01:45,898
Was soll denn die Scheiße?
Bist du denn verrückt geworden?

895
01:01:46,119 --> 01:01:48,400
Mit diesem Scheißgewehr hättest
du mich erschießen können!

896
01:01:48,455 --> 01:01:51,049
Du hast so nah an mir vorbeigeschossen!

897
01:01:51,166 --> 01:01:53,544
Was heißt hier Entschuldigung. Du bist woh!
Verrückt ge Worden!

898
01:01:53,627 --> 01:01:55,107
SOLDIER: Zurückbleiben! Zurückbleiben!

899
01:02:01,051 --> 01:02:03,975
Nicht sprechen, nicht sprechen!
Einfach zu den Lastwagen gehen!

900
01:02:04,804 --> 01:02:07,398
Weiterlaufen! Weiterlaufen! Nicht sprechen!

901
01:02:07,807 --> 01:02:08,808
(WHISTLE BLOWING)

902
01:02:17,609 --> 01:02:19,737
Komm her, du Judensau!

903
01:02:19,819 --> 01:02:21,696
Bleib stehen, hab ich gesagt. Bleib stehen!

904
01:02:24,866 --> 01:02:26,906
(WOMAN WHISPERING IN POLISH)
(WOMAN SPEAKING POLISH)

905
01:02:27,911 --> 01:02:29,630
Chodzcie tu szybko!

906
01:02:53,061 --> 01:02:54,233
(DOOR BURSTS OPEN)

907
01:03:19,504 --> 01:03:21,802
She's good as dead. Leave her.

908
01:03:21,881 --> 01:03:25,260
Give me a moment. Just let me bring her inside.

909
01:03:27,095 --> 01:03:28,597
(PEOPLE SCREAMING)

910
01:03:28,722 --> 01:03:31,145
(MAN ANNOUNCING IN GERMAN ON PA)

911
01:03:31,224 --> 01:03:34,694
You can join her or join the line.

912
01:03:38,064 --> 01:03:39,657
(WHISTLE BLOWING)

913
01:03:39,733 --> 01:03:40,950
Women to the left!

914
01:03:41,067 --> 01:03:42,068
- Wilhelm!
- Rose!

915
01:03:42,152 --> 01:03:44,996
Men to right. Women to the left.
(WOMAN PROTESTING)

916
01:03:45,071 --> 01:03:46,288
Women to the left.

917
01:03:46,406 --> 01:03:47,806
Let me go! I won't leave my husband!

918
01:03:52,746 --> 01:03:54,307
Women to the left, and men to the right.

919
01:03:54,414 --> 01:03:55,961
Promise me, be safe.

920
01:03:58,877 --> 01:04:00,845
Your government is in the wrong country!

921
01:04:00,920 --> 01:04:03,799
(SHOUTING CONTINUES)

922
01:04:03,923 --> 01:04:07,097
(WAILING)

923
01:04:07,719 --> 01:04:09,266
Mama! Mama!

924
01:04:10,889 --> 01:04:12,937
(PEOPLE SHOUTING)

925
01:04:23,777 --> 01:04:25,620
(DOGS BARKING)

926
01:04:26,696 --> 01:04:27,868
(SOLDIERS SHOUTING)

927
01:04:27,947 --> 01:04:30,291
(MACHINE GUN FIRE)

928
01:04:57,435 --> 01:04:58,428
Mila!

929
01:05:18,540 --> 01:05:20,963
(BELL CHIMING)

930
01:05:25,672 --> 01:05:26,674
(SCREAMS)

931
01:05:32,679 --> 01:05:34,101
You have a Blauschein.

932
01:05:34,222 --> 01:05:35,644
Blauschein doesn't matter anymore,

933
01:05:35,723 --> 01:05:37,270
they are cleaning out the ghetto.

934
01:05:37,350 --> 01:05:38,897
There is not enough room for you.

935
01:05:39,018 --> 01:05:41,011
What are you talking about? We tried it before.

936
01:05:41,011 --> 01:05:42,355
There's more than enough room.

937
01:05:42,814 --> 01:05:44,631
I changed my mind!

938
01:05:44,790 --> 01:05:45,507
Mama!

939
01:05:45,692 --> 01:05:47,911
Oh, look at the space in there.

940
01:05:48,027 --> 01:05:50,280
Now look at me. You are just scared.

941
01:05:50,363 --> 01:05:52,457
I can fit the girl, but not you.

942
01:05:52,532 --> 01:05:53,624
Mama, I'm coming out!

943
01:05:53,741 --> 01:05:54,833
Stay where you are.

944
01:05:54,909 --> 01:05:57,162
I'd rather you be here than who knows where.

945
01:05:57,245 --> 01:05:58,242
Mama!

946
01:06:03,042 --> 01:06:04,840
(SOLDIERS SHOUTING IN GERMAN)

947
01:06:06,880 --> 01:06:08,348
(DOGS BARKING)

948
01:06:19,792 --> 01:06:22,145
Herr Kommandant!
(SOLDIERS LAUGHING)

949
01:06:22,228 --> 01:06:23,730
Very good saluting.

950
01:06:23,813 --> 01:06:25,736
I respectfully report I've been given orders

951
01:06:25,857 --> 01:06:27,780
to clear the bundles from the road,

952
01:06:27,859 --> 01:06:30,508
so there will be no obstructions to the thoroughfare.

953
01:06:30,987 --> 01:06:35,538
Finish and join the lines, little Polish clicking soldier.

954
01:06:35,617 --> 01:06:36,869
(CHUCKLES)

955
01:06:53,760 --> 01:06:54,761
(BLOWS WHISTLE)

956
01:06:58,932 --> 01:07:00,354
Mrs. Dresner?

957
01:07:00,433 --> 01:07:01,376
Yes.

958
01:07:02,185 --> 01:07:04,358
Oh, you are a friend of my son's.

959
01:07:04,604 --> 01:07:07,027
Don't worry, I'll go quietly. I'll be no trouble.

960
01:07:07,106 --> 01:07:10,379
No, get under the stairs. They'll be done searching soon.

961
01:07:10,400 --> 01:07:11,573
You'll be safe there.

962
01:07:11,653 --> 01:07:13,200
Please hide.

963
01:07:13,321 --> 01:07:14,868
- ...warmed by the sun.
- Please.

964
01:07:14,948 --> 01:07:16,471
Hide under the stairs.

965
01:07:20,578 --> 01:07:23,001
BOY: I've searched the building. There's no one here.

966
01:07:23,081 --> 01:07:24,309
Also, gehen wir zum nächsten Block.

967
01:07:24,358 --> 01:07:25,310
Einverstanden.

968
01:07:33,091 --> 01:07:34,638
- Danka!
- Mama!

969
01:07:35,510 --> 01:07:36,511
(PANTING)

970
01:07:40,765 --> 01:07:42,108
Hello, Adam.

971
01:07:43,726 --> 01:07:45,979
Hello, Danka.

972
01:07:51,609 --> 01:07:54,008
Come with me. I will put you in the good line.

973
01:07:55,113 --> 01:07:57,161
(SOLDIERS SHOUTING IN DISTANCE)

974
01:07:57,240 --> 01:08:00,414
Do you know the saying, "An hour of life is still life"?

975
01:08:00,493 --> 01:08:04,589
You are not a boy anymore. I'm saying a blessing for you.

976
01:08:07,625 --> 01:08:10,720
(PEOPLE SHOUTING)
(MACHINE GUNS FIRING)

977
01:08:15,341 --> 01:08:17,685
